\section{3D Shape Descriptors}

In this section, we outline six basic global shape descriptors as well as five distinct shape descriptors used for 3D model feature extraction. These descriptors provide statistical representations of an object's geometry. The goal of these descriptions is to provide features that can be used to compare 3D models. Before all the features are calculated, the shapes are first processed. In this process, we fill the holes that shapes might have to make them watertight, and we fix the windings of the triangles to all wind in the same direction.

To validate the performance and interpretability of each descriptor, we evaluate them on four distinct 3D shapes: a \textit{round} shape (hot air balloon), a \textit{flat} shape (cellphone), an \textit{elongated} shape (bottle), and an \textit{irregular} shape (cow). These reference shapes allow us to assess whether each descriptor behaves as expected across different geometric categories. The shapes are depicted in figure X.

