\subsection{Basic Global Descriptors}
These descriptors provide quick and simple measurements of an object’s size, shape, and boundaries. For each descriptor, we describe the purpose, calculation, and how it can be applied for feature comparison.

\subsubsection{Surface Area}
\textbf{Purpose:} Measures the total area of the object’s outer surface, which helps to understand its overall size and complexity.\\
\textbf{Calculation:} Add up the areas of all the triangular faces in the object's mesh.\\
\textbf{Usage:} Can be used to normalise other size-related metrics or as a basic filter for searching by size.

\subsubsection{Diameter}
\textbf{Purpose:} Defines the largest distance between any two vertices on the object.\\
\textbf{Calculation:} Find the maximum distance between all pairs of vertices in the mesh.\\
\textbf{Usage:} Can be used as a measurement of the object's overall size.

\subsubsection{Compactness and Sphericity}
\textbf{Purpose:} Indicates how similar the object's shape is to a sphere, with values close to 1 implying a compact, perfectly round shape. We normalise this compactness value to get the sphericity. Sphericity sets the value of compactness between 0 and 1, with one representing a perfect sphere and all other shapes having a value less than one.\\
\textbf{Calculation:}
\[
\text{Compactness} = \frac{A^3}{36 \pi V^2}
\]
To calculate the sphericity:
\[
\text{Sphericity} = \frac{1}{\text{Compactness}}
\]
\textbf{Usage:} Helps distinguish between spherical shapes and elongated or flat shapes.

\subsubsection{3D Rectangularity}
\textbf{Purpose:} Measures how well the object fits into a rectangular box. A value of 1.0 indicates a perfect fit.\\
\textbf{Calculation:}
\[
\text{Rectangularity} = \frac{V}{V_{\text{OBB}}}
\]
\textbf{Usage:} Useful for identifying blocky shapes versus irregular ones.

\subsubsection{Convexity}
\textbf{Purpose:} Measures the smoothness of the object's surface. A fully convex shape has a value of 1.0, indicating no indentations.\\
\textbf{Calculation:}
\[
\text{Convexity} = \frac{V}{V_{\text{hull}}}
\]
\textbf{Usage:} Effective for differentiating solid shapes with a high convexity from complex or hollow objects with a low convexity.

\subsubsection{Eccentricity}
\textbf{Purpose:} Measures how uniform the mass of an object is in all directions. A compact, round object indicates even mass distribution, while elongated or flat objects suggest uneven distribution.\\
\textbf{Calculation:}
\[
\text{Eccentricity} = \frac{\lambda_{\min}}{\lambda_{\max}}
\]
where $\lambda_{\min}$ and $\lambda_{\max}$ are the smallest and largest eigenvalues of the covariance matrix, respectively.\\
\textbf{Usage:} Useful for distinguishing evenly distributed shapes from uneven ones.

\subsection{Comparative Validation of Fundamental Descriptors}
To understand these global metrics, performance across different shape categories based on their characteristics is examined as depicted in Figure~X. A round shape (hot air balloon), a flat shape (cellphone), an elongated shape (bottle), and an irregular shape (cow) are compared to each other to see if the different descriptors match what we expect.

\textbf{Round Shapes:} These shapes are highly uniform and should result in high sphericity (approaching 1) and high eccentricity (also near 1). They should show lower values for both convexity and rectangularity. This matches what we see in Figure~X.

\textbf{Elongated Shapes:} These shapes should exhibit high rectangularity, as they resemble their oriented bounding box. This is the case for the elongated bottle, which scores the highest in rectangularity.

\textbf{Irregular Shapes:} These shapes commonly register lower scores for convexity and 3D rectangularity due to multiple concavities. This is visible in Figure~X, where the irregularly shaped cow scores low on convexity.

\textbf{Convex Shapes:} These should match their convex hull. Rectangular and elongated shapes are expected to be highly convex, as seen in Figure~X.

